Before — one class owns pricing, notifications, and persistence for an order:

```java
class OrderManager {
    void completeOrder(Order o) {
        double total = o.getItems().stream().mapToDouble(Item::getPrice).sum();
        if (o.getCustomer().getTier() == Tier.GOLD) total *= 0.9;
        db.save(o.withTotal(total));
        emailClient.send(o.getCustomer().getEmail(), "Order confirmed", total);
        auditLog.record("order_completed", o.getId());
    }
}
```

After — each responsibility moves to a class that owns it, and `OrderManager` only coordinates:

```java
class OrderManager {
    void completeOrder(Order o) {
        double total = pricing.calculateTotal(o);
        repository.save(o.withTotal(total));
        notifier.sendConfirmation(o, total);
        auditLog.record("order_completed", o.getId());
    }
}
```

`PricingService`, `OrderRepository`, and `OrderNotifier` can now each be tested, changed, and reasoned about alone — nothing in `OrderManager` needs to know *how* a total is calculated, only that `pricing` can calculate one.
